If your JSON-based UI for AI agents is collapsing or breaking during rendering, it is likely due to a breakdown in the "contract" between the agent's output and the frontend's expectations. This typically manifests as parsing errors, unhandled null values, or invalid syntax in the streamed response.
Common reasons for layout collapse include:
1. Incomplete JSON during Streaming
When an AI agent streams a response, it sends the JSON character by character. If your frontend tries to JSON.parse() the message before the agent has finished sending it, the code will throw a SyntaxError and the component will crash. 
The Fix: Use a partial JSON parser (like partial-json-parser) or a library like CopilotKit or A2UI that handles incomplete streams and only updates the UI when a valid fragment or full object is available.
2. Format & Mapping Errors
AI agents can be inconsistent. They might include unescaped characters, trailing commas, or wrap the JSON in Markdown backticks (e.g., ```json ... ```), which breaks standard parsers. 
The Fix:
Sanitize the raw response string by stripping out Markdown indicators before parsing.
Use Pydantic (Python) or Zod (TypeScript) to validate the structure. If the data doesn't match the schema, provide a fallback UI instead of letting the component fail. 
3. Missing or Unexpected Keys
If your UI component expects a field like props.button_text but the AI agent returns label, your component will likely render undefined or crash if it tries to perform an operation on that missing field. 
The Fix: Set default values for every prop in your dynamic components. Never assume the agent will provide 100% of the required data. 
4. Overloaded or Vague Prompts
If your system prompt is too "flexible," the agent may hallucinate different structures for the same task. This "multipolar behavior" makes the UI feel unstable because the layout changes randomly between runs. 
The Fix: Use Few-Shot Prompting. Give the agent 3–5 specific examples of the exact JSON structure it must return for different scenarios. 
5. Context Loss & Timing Issues
If the agent is managing multiple turns of conversation, it may "forget" previous UI state requirements, causing the next JSON payload to be missing critical layout instructions. 
The Fix: Maintain a shared state between the agent and frontend. Instead of the agent generating the entire UI every time, have it only send "updates" to a persistent state object that the frontend renders. 
Summary Checklist for Stability
Sanitize: Strip Markdown backticks and whitespace from the agent's output before parsing.
Validate: Use a schema validator like Zod to catch structural errors early.
Fallback: Wrap your dynamic renderer in a React Error Boundary to prevent the entire app from crashing if one component fails.
Trace: Use tools like LangSmith or Helicone to see exactly what JSON the agent sent when the crash occurred. 
